Hi Philip, Colin, Chris, Matthias, Kirk, David, Xiangyuan LI,

KIP-944 was extended a bit more to explain why effect systems like Zio=20
and Cats-effects make it impossible to run code on a specific thread.

I understand that using an effect system is pretty far removed from=20
writing Java in transaction script style, the style that is probably=20
used by most Kafka committers. I took me quite some time to get=20
comfortable with effects. It is not the academic fringe tool as=20
perceived by many. For me it is a way to quickly and correctly write=20
serious data processing applications.

Even so, we both use the same Kafka eco-system and supporting different=20
styles only makes it more rich. IMHO it would be a shame if we can not=20
live together using the same code base.

Philip, thanks for your support. I hope I have convinced the others as=20
well by now. If not, I am giving up and I will spend my energy elsewhere.

Kind regards,
 =C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 Erik.


Op 24-07-2023 om 18:12 schreef Erik van Oosten:
> Hello Xiangyuan LI,
>
> I am not familiar with coroutines, nor with Kotlin. You will have to=20
> work with the documentation:=20
> https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coroutines-overview.html
>
> However, I am familiar with Zio and Cats-effects (both Scala=20
> libraries). In both Zio and Cats-effects one creates effects (aka=20
> workflows) which are descriptions of a computation. For example, when=20
> executing the Scala code `val effect =3D ZIO.attempt(println("Hello=20
> world!"))` one creates only a description; it does not print anything=20
> yet. The language to describe these effects is rich enough to describe=20
> entire applications including things like concurrency. In fact, the=20
> language is so rich, that it is the most convenient way that I know to=20
> safely write highly concurrent and async applications. For many=20
> developer teams the performance penalty (which is real but not big) is=20
> worth it.
>
> To execute a Zio or Cats effect one gives it to the runtime. The=20
> runtime then schedules the work on one of the threads in its=20
> thread-pool. Zio, nor Cats-effects supports running an effect on the=20
> thread that manages the thread-pool.
>
> I hope this clear enough.
>
> Kind regards,
> =C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 Erik.
